Mojo hardware and runtime qualification for 5BSD
==============================================
Updated 2026-10-09. The targets include Intel/AMD CPUs, AMD GPU compute,
and Raspberry Pi 4 and 5 CPUs and VideoCore GPUs. Intel GPU feasibility
is separate from Intel CPU support. Pi hardware testing is the last step,
at the user's request; no Pi test machine is available yet.

CPU status
----------
The native amd64 Mojo suite covers SIMD, SIMD fast-math, vectorization,
atomics, ownership, collections, C ABI, Python integration and compiler
regressions. This is not a claim that every CPU feature is qualified.
See README.mojo-tests.5BSD for runtime, Python harness, debugger, NUMA,
sanitizer and other remaining coverage gaps. The current execution host
is an AMD Ryzen 7 255; Intel hardware has not been exercised separately.
SIMD tests passing does not establish every AVX-512 or ARM intrinsic.

Pi 4: Cortex-A72; Pi 5: Cortex-A76. Cross-compilation probes emit
FreeBSD AArch64 objects with scalar addition and four-lane floating-point
NEON FMA. The probe checks object ABI and disassembly. It does not link
an ARM64 program or execute one. lang/mojo remains amd64-only until a
native compiler/runtime build, package validation and test run qualify
ARM64. Do not infer Pi support from the availability of an LLVM target.

Native Pi qualification must cover the compiler/runtime build with all
available logical CPUs, platform/ABI checks, standard-library/compiler
tests, packaged examples, Python interop, NEON execution, installation
and removal in a disposable environment. Use the ports checkout selected
with PORTSDIR and /usr/obj for staging. System builds, if needed, use
/usr/src's maintained targets; this work does not authorize an upgrade
or reboot. Record each Pi model and the exact base/kernel/firmware versions.

Accelerator dependency boundaries
--------------------------------
All GPU paths need a compiler backend, device/runtime bindings, a working
kernel driver, and user-space libraries. A newer base LLVM or Mesa package
alone cannot supply all of these pieces.

AMD: upstream Mojo uses HIP (libamdhip64.so). A native ROCm path also
requires its device compilation/runtime dependencies and kernel compute
interfaces. This host has amdgpu, Mesa RADV and a Radeon 780M, but no
installed HIP/HSA runtime or /dev/kfd. No native Mojo GPU test passes yet.
The source-built Mojo compiler reports no registered accelerators.

Intel: the CPU compiler's x86 backend supports Intel CPU targets. Intel
integrated and discrete GPUs are both absent from Mojo's documented GPU
backends. Mesa ANV/Vulkan support for a particular GPU is a separate
capability. An Intel Mojo GPU path would require new backend/runtime work;
adding Intel GPU libraries to the CPU port would not enable it.

Pi: Mesa V3D/V3DV supports Pi 4/5 rendering and Vulkan on supported stacks.
It depends on the V3D kernel DRM driver; VC4 provides display support.
No V3D implementation was found in the checked-out /usr/src/sys/dev or
/usr/src/sys/modules, and the current mesa-dri port does not offer V3DV.
Native kernel-driver integration and a Mojo backend/runtime remain work
items. Packaging only Mesa's user-space driver would be insufficient.

Other upstream GPU platforms: NVIDIA uses CUDA driver libraries and its
device compilation backend; Apple uses Metal on macOS. Neither implies
that the corresponding native 5BSD implementation is present.

MAX library probes
------------------
The upstream comptime_subexpression.mojo documentation example compiled
and ran using the max/mojo and max/kernels/src include paths. This proves
a CPU Layout use case can work directly from source.

The shared-lib-c-host/test_shared_lib_runtime_init.mojo test compiled as
a shared object, but dlopen failed with missing
AsyncRT_DeviceContext_create. Thus compilation alone does not qualify
MAX parallelism. The checkout imports AsyncRTMojoBindings and libmax
from upstream platform wheels; their required implementation is not
available as a native FreeBSD library in this build. No no-op bindings
or foreign-OS library aliases should be substituted to report a pass.

AMD runtime work is the GPU priority
-----------------------------------
The requested integration is native Mojo GPU kernels through AMD's supported
runtime interfaces, with the required libraries ported to 5BSD. The
experimental Mojo-to-llama.cpp bridge has been withdrawn from the port at the
user's request. Standalone llama.cpp/GGML results are separate application
experiments and do not qualify Mojo, MAX, HIP, HSA, or AMD kernel execution.
Their logs are preserved under /usr/obj/mojo-port-validation/2026-10-09/.

The remaining chain is Mojo's HIP interface, the HIP runtime, the ROCr/HSA
runtime and thunk, and native AMD compute-driver support. Compiler support
libraries and device bitcode also need matching versions. Existing Vulkan
or amdgpu graphics support does not establish that compute-driver interface.
GPU execution/retries remain deferred by the user; source and build work may
continue. Raspberry Pi 4/5 hardware validation remains last.

Existing FreeBSD compute work reviewed (2026-10-09)
-------------------------------------------------
The likely CUDA project is https://github.com/NapoleonWils0n/freebsd-cuda.
It packages Linux applications using Linuxulator/Podman and NVIDIA libraries.
The separate https://github.com/shkhln/libc6-shim permits limited glibc ABI
compatibility in native FreeBSD processes. Both are NVIDIA prior art; neither
establishes AMD HIP support. The current ports tree already contains
emulators/libc6-shim and science/linux-ai-ml-env. The latter's UVM shim returns
NV_ERR_NOT_SUPPORTED for UVM initialization and redirects /dev/nvidia-uvm to
/dev/null. It is not an implementation of GPU queues or AMD's KFD interface.

AMD explicitly requires a host compute driver even for Linux containers:
https://rocm.docs.amd.com/projects/install-on-linux/en/docs-6.0.0/how-to/docker.html
Its ROCr thunk opens /dev/kfd and reads KFD topology. Linux compatibility does
not by itself supply these interfaces. Native runtime porting remains the
primary path; a Linux ABI path would need its own driver/ABI qualification.

Reusable native work is reported by the FreeBSD project:
https://www.freebsd.org/status/report-2026-04-2026-06/rocm/
It links https://github.com/Yohello1/rocm-systems and
https://github.com/Yohello1/drm-kmod-rocm/tree/super-special.
The report describes toolchain/runtime changes and partial amdkfd support;
it does not establish complete driver operation or Radeon 780M qualification.
Runtime branch to_merge_in at 87ef7d63460c515e7b202d5ea971ebb6fbbf2faa
contains FreeBSD-specific libhsakmt build changes and is being reviewed.
Driver branch super-special at f40bce3958fe0c604b6c74f96c3ab637d3a16de6
is a source-review candidate, not a driver selected for installation.
The existing drm-6.12.85_3 hardware build source contains an amdkfd Makefile,
but its amd/Makefile does not enable the amdkfd subdirectory.

Review and qualification order: native thunk/HSA runtime dependencies and
OS interfaces; matching HIP/CLR and device compiler libraries; compute-driver
integration against the intended kernel; then device enumeration, allocation,
copy, queues, events and real Mojo kernels. GPU execution remains deferred.
No CUDA compatibility experiment has been installed or run on this host.

Initial native thunk configuration stops at the missing libnuma dependency.
Source review also found incomplete FreeBSD include selection, Linux sysfs
topology assumptions, unsupported performance counters and an explicit SVM
ABI TODO in the FreeBSD KFD header. These patches are reusable prior art, not
a completed ROCm port. Evidence: /usr/obj/mojo-rocm-validation/initial-audit.json.

Native AMD source progress (2026-10-09, 20:30 UTC)
-----------------------------------------------
The private source prototype fixes FreeBSD header selection, a missing status
return in an unsupported counter API, a modern libdrm type collision and the
ioctl size-field limit. FreeBSD uses a 13-bit size field (8191 bytes); the
CPU-only bounds test covers the maximum 1020 SVM attributes and the first
oversized request. Nineteen of twenty thunk translation units pass compiler
syntax checks using installed dependencies. Supplying the prior NUMA shim
headers and fixing the same ioctl-size check in fmm.c allows all twenty to
compile to objects. The shim implementation has not been accepted or linked.
The object-symbol audit also finds a missing FreeBSD implementation of
hsakmt_kfdcontext_init_perf_context. These results do not establish a linked
runtime, kernel ABI qualification or GPU execution.
Patch and checks: /usr/obj/mojo-rocm-validation/.

The candidate driver contains kfd_freebsd_stubs.c with missing debugger/event
implementations and empty dispatch tables for several GPU generations. The
runtime still expects Linux sysfs topology. These are engineering gaps, not
package dependencies that can be solved just by installing Linux libraries.

The pinned Mojo checkout registers HostTraits, HostLowering and HostBackend;
accelerator requests explicitly require MAX. This build has no native MAX
accelerator/compiler integration. HIP/ROCr/KFD porting is necessary but does
not by itself add the missing Mojo GPU backend. Do not bypass the MAX check
or substitute no-op bindings as evidence of accelerator support.

Candidate runtime/driver ioctl audit
------------------------------------
CPU-only header probes found 38 matching command encodings and one mismatch:
CREATE_PROCESS is 0xc0084b27 in the runtime but 0x20004b27 in the candidate
driver. Seven additional runtime commands are absent from the driver header.
The candidate kfd_chardev.c also has no CREATE_PROCESS handler. The runtime
expects the secondary-context descriptor through an output structure; blindly
changing its number to match the driver would not implement that behavior.
Evidence: /usr/obj/mojo-rocm-validation/kfd-abi/result.json. Matching command
numbers do not prove structure offsets, kernel behavior or hardware support.

NUMA dependency and newer upstream notes (2026-10-09)
----------------------------------------------------
The prior NUMA shim is https://codeberg.org/yohwllo/FreeBSD_libnuma.
Reviewed tree: b1f1f146860e6f688577097367c138e6b2c327d6. Its mbind implementation
unconditionally returns success without implementing address-range policy.
Its source defines num_bitmask_setbit, while its header and ROCr callers use
numa_bitmask_setbit. It is not a qualified native runtime dependency.
The source is preserved for review with verified Git blob hashes under
/usr/obj/mojo-rocm-validation/prior-art/numa-source/; it was not installed.
Object compilation and unresolved-symbol evidence: thunk-objects/result.json.

The author's August 22 notes describe RDNA3/Radeon 780M driver bring-up and
partial KFD communication, explicitly still nonfunctional runtime operation:
https://csclub.uwaterloo.ca/~s23adhik/myPosts/ROCmFreeBSD_pt6.html
They are a useful newer baseline than the initial runtime-build report, but
are not evidence of executing HIP or Mojo kernels. Their installation commands
are not the 5BSD build workflow and have not been executed here. Kernel changes
must be reviewed against /usr/src and integrated through the maintained build
and hardware package targets before any separately authorized installation.
